  "raw": " the band’s last album, as guitarist Brian May and keyboardist Roger Taylor both suffered serious injuries in a car accident shortly after recording it. That meant that guitarist John Deacon would have to be hired to replace May that summer.\n\nQueen were a much different band at this time compared to those that followed. The ‘70s was a time for hard rock and heavy metal, with David Bowie leading the charge, but Queen’s sound was one of a more melodic, more sophisticated prog rock. The group even experimented with synthesizers at some point, a genre that was still nascent when the group emerged.\n\nDespite some critical flak for playing a pop/rock album, A Night at the Opera sold well and became a cult record which saw a resurgence in late-‘70s popularity.\n\nQueen’s latest studio album is also due this summer.\n\n7. The Doors\n\nReleased: Nov. 5, 1968\n\n",
  "serialized": "the band’s last album, as guitarist Brian May and keyboardist Roger Taylor both suffered serious injuries in a car accident shortly after recording it. That meant that guitarist John Deacon would have to be hired to replace May that summer. Queen were a much different band at this time compared to those that followed. The ‘70s was a time for hard rock and heavy metal, with David Bowie leading the charge, but Queen’s sound was one of a more melodic, more sophisticated prog rock. The group even experimented with synthesizers at some point, a genre that was still nascent when the group emerged. Despite some critical flak for playing a pop/rock album, A Night at the Opera sold well and became a cult record which saw a resurgence in late-‘70s popularity. Queen’s latest studio album is also due this summer. 7. The Doors Released: Nov. 5, 1968",
